# Access and Credentials

## Context

Workspace membership, project/server assignments and explicit environment grants jointly determine access. Admin observation does not imply deploy or approve permission.

## Decisions

- Scope resource reads and mutations to active workspace membership. Check both route middleware and policies/services; preserve checks on logs, polling and background execution as well as page actions.
- Keep viewers unable to deploy/approve, inactive or disabled members unable to act, and production approvers independent of requesters. Do not replace explicit grants with role shortcuts.
- When moving an environment to a new server, identify members missing that server assignment. Require deliberate access transfer before changing the target, preserve environment rights and record transferred user IDs in the audit event. Never grant new server access silently.
- Validate environment-name uniqueness within the project and path uniqueness within the server, ignoring the current environment on edits. Preserve database constraints and serialize same-project name changes.
- Verify host trust before decrypting credentials or authenticating. Peer and credential changes invalidate pending candidates; peer changes also clear the pinned fingerprint.
- Keep private keys/passphrases encrypted, hidden from serialization, absent from job payloads and excluded from flashed validation input. Do not log or display decrypted material.
- Privileged HTTP actions require enrolled and session-verified MFA; workers independently check enrollment. Preserve TOTP replay prevention, session rotation and local administrative recovery.

## Verification

- Test cross-workspace access, viewers, explicit grants, revocation, MFA gates and serialization exclusions when changing permissions or identity.
- Test server reassignment with missing grants and explicit transfer, and duplicate-name validation for both creation and edits.
